\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The goal of this template is to guide you through the creation of your thesis.
 A bunch of people did already their theses with me and it turned out that they often have comparable questions (and my answers are comparable,
 too).
 Hence,
 this document should help you understanding how the structure of your thesis should look like and what parts of your thesis belong to what parts in the document.
 And in case you consider this document as not sufficient:
 contact your supervisor (who is probably me:
 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
mailto:stefan.hanenberg@uni-due.de
\end_layout

\end_inset

) – and do it right from the beginning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Before you start:
 do you write your thesis in German or English?
 In case you write it in German,
 set the document language to German (Dokument -> Einstellungen -> Sprache) and check whether the generated keywords (content,
 list of tables,
 etc.
 are in german as well as the figure and table name as well).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By the way,
 this document is written quite quickly and contains probably countless typos.
 Please do not hesitate to contact me in case you found bugs – the next students (and me) will appreciate your contribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Start writing about the context of the work.
 In case a certain techniques is being studies in this thesis (and it would be rather strange if 
\series bold
not
\series default
 a certain technique is not being studied in your thesis),
 in what situations is it applied?
 How relevant is this technique?
 Is this technique relevant for teaching,
 industry or research?
 If you write for example something about a programming language feature in Java,
 it makes sense to introduce into programming,
 the relevance of Java and the language construct itself.
 When was it introduced and what was the motivation?
 Typically,
 you will use a bunch of references in the introduction.
 For example if you write about a language construct in Java,
 there are references about programming,
 Java and the language construct.
 A general guideline is,
 that technical references such as URLs should be rather found in footnotes
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This is an example of a footnote use a 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
http://myreference.com
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since I am the supervisor of you thesis,
 you probably do a controlled experiment.
 Hence,
 you should also have in the introduction why running such an experiment is necessary.
 From the background perspective,
 it probably makes sense to write 1-2 paragraphs about why experimentation is necessary at all.
 You probably will refer to works such as Kaijanaho 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Kaijanaho:2015:Dissertation"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 Ko et al.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Ko:2015:PracticalGuideToControlledExperiments"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 Vegas et al.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "VegasApaJuristo2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 or Stefik et al.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Stefik_FoundationOfEvidence_2014"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 who state how many experiments can be found in programming language research or software engineering.
 Do not blindly use those references – check,
 whether the statements you want make can be justified with these references.
 And double-check whether you find newer literature (the previous ones are already quite old).
 You might also want to use some papers that complain about missing empirical studies in the literature (such as 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Hanenberg_FaithHopeLove_2010,StefikHanenberg_PLWars_2014,StefikHanenberg_MethodIrrularities_2017"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 References are written down in the file 
\family typewriter
bibliography.bib
\family default
.
 By the way:
 you almost never add a bibtex-entry by hand.
 Go to webpages such as dblp,
 Google Scholar,
 ACM DL,
 etc.
 – they give you the bibtex entries that you can just copy (maybe you need to edit the names after copying).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Maybe you even want to make a broader introduction into empirical studies – and even want somephilosophical background 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "popper-scientificdiscovery"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In the end,
 you are relatively free in the introduction.
 
\emph on
But keep in mind:
 the goal is to introduce the reader into the topic.
 The goal ist NOT that you write down everything you know.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Typically,
 introductions are quite short – if it requires more than 3-5 pages,
 there is probably something wrong with it and you should take into account putting some text fragments into other sections.
 But it is also possible that you decide to put the background and the research question as subchapters in the introduction.
 It slightly depends on the flow of the text:
 if the background is rather long,
 make a separate chapter out of it.
\end_layout

