\begin_layout Subsection
Randomized control trials
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this section,
 you probably speak about randomized control trials,
 or you speak about other kinds of trials,
 depending on what you do in your work.
 You should introduce into experimentation in general.
 Literature about experimentation in software engineering is for example the book by Wohlin et al.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Wohlin_ExperimentationInSoftwareEngineer_2012"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 or the book by Juristo and Moreno 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "JuristoMoreno_ExperimentationSoftwareEngineering_2010"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 but papers such as the one by Kitchenham et al.
 might help as well 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kitchenham2008evaluating"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The goal of this section is give readers all information he needs to understand the experiments you did in in this thesis.
 It probably makes sense to speak about 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
dependent and independent variables,
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
experiment layouts,
 experiment designs,
 maybe about protocols,
 and 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
analysis methods (which typically means inference statistics) – actually,
 you need to speak about those things that you are about to explain in this document.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example,
 when our ran a crossover trial as part of this thesis,
 you do not want to write later on in a section 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

by the way,
 we did a crossover trial
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

:
 then,
 there is a need to introduce crossover trials in the background chapter.
 When you did not ran a crossover trial,
 there is no need to mention it (again – the goal is not that you write down everything you know:
 the goal is to write a thesis that contains what is needed,
 but not more,
 this is a scientific text,
 not prose).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Introduce into the analysis methods that appear later on.
 Introduce into the kind of numbers that you need later on (p-values,
 effect sizes,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{2}_{p}$
\end_inset

,
 etc,).
 Also,
 you probably need to introduce into the logic of experimentation,
 i.e.
 you probably need to write down what the test for a p-value means and why it has to be compared against some α level,
 etc.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There might be situations where you cannot argue why certain things are done in a certain way.
 In the most easist way,
 it might be unclear for you (for good reasons) why an α level of .05 is used.
 At a certain point,
 you can just say 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

this is a value that is commonly used in the literature
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 But now,
 you should either refer to a paper that gives evidence that this value is commonly used in the literature,
 or you should enumerate a number of papers that do it that way.
 
\series bold
Do not just write down 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

author X said this is quite common in the literature
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You cannot describe everything in detail.
 For example,
 when your experiment is analysed using a t-test,
 give a brief description of the t-test,
 but then give additional literature where to find more.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I know,
 it is quite boring to write this stuff down,
 especially since I handed you already a bunch oh theses that have such a section.
 It probably feels for you as if you write stuff down that was already written down somewhere else.
 Please still do it – most readers in software science do not have a background in experimentation and it makes sense to write such things down for them.
\end_layout

