\begin_layout Standard
The effect of indentation length was substantial:
 an indentation length of 2 was,
 on average,
 94.3% slower than a length of 8,
 and still 45.8% slower than a length of 6.
 This gap narrowed considerably between the longer lengths,
 with length 6 being only 9.6% slower than length 8.
 This suggests that the benefit of increasing indentation length diminishes quickly,
 with most of the improvement occurring between very short and moderate lengths,
 rather than continuing at the same rate for longer lengths.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The estimated marginal means for Nesting_Level showed a pronounced inverted U-shaped pattern,
 with the slowest reaction times occurring around the center of the nested structure,
 level 5 was 60.3% slower than level 1 and 60.3% slower than level 9.
 Levels 6 and 7 were similarly slow,
 while the outermost levels on both ends were read comparatively quickly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
