\begin_layout Subsection
Discussion
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Discussion"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This experiment shows that a higher Indentation_Length was,
 in this setup,
 consistently associated with faster reaction times than the previous,
 shorter length.
 While reaction times did decrease substantially as indentation length increased,
 the size of this improvement was highly uneven.
 The pattern resembles a diminishing return:
 the difference between a length of 2 and a length of 4 was far larger than the difference between a length of 6 and a length of 8,
 suggesting that the benefit of additional indentation falls off rather than continuing linearly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The strong interaction between indentation length and nesting level indicates that indentation length does not affect all nesting levels equally.
 For a short indentation length,
 conditions near the center of the nested structure took over 170% longer to process than conditions at the outer levels,
 whereas this discrepancy was much smaller for longer indentation lengths.
 This suggests that some structural property of the code,
 related to a condition's position within the nesting hierarchy,
 interacts with indentation length in a way that no single indentation length could uniformly account for.
 This experiment therefore represents the empirical starting point of this thesis.
 Why nesting level and indentation length interact so strongly is a question that could not yet be fully answered at this stage.
\end_layout

