\begin_layout Subsection
Discussion
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Discussion-1_Chained_Condtions_Preliminary"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results replicate the general pattern from the baseline experiment (See Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Discussion"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) but providing important insights ,
 because increasing the indentation length does not straightforwardly translate into faster reading times.
 While length 8 was again the fastest condition and length 2 the slowest,
 the intermediate lengths did not decrease monotonically.
 Length 6 was slower than length 4.
 This breaks the assumption that 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

more indentation is always better
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 One explanation could be that once a moderate indentation length is reached,
 further increase may impose their own costs.
 For example,
 wider horizontal spacing could require more eye movement per line or push code closer to the edge of comfortable visual scanning and the participant could need more time.
 But Length 8 is still faster than 6.
 Another explanation could be that some incidental properties of the randomly generated conditions,
 such as word length or visual homogeneity,
 could have played a role .
 What can be said is that a higher indentation length as a standalone factor is not always beneficial,
 but it is important to be said that the current design,
 given the moderate sample size (N = 45 per condition),
 cannot fully disentangle this effect from measurement noise.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Dynamic Indentation Preliminary Experiment
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Dynamic-Indentation-Preliminary"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The previous experiment showed that indentation length can affect reading time in a non-linear way,
 with a notable deviation at an indentation length of six.
 This raises the question of whether a fixed indentation length is well suited to represent the structure of nested if-statements at all.
 This preliminary experiment introduces dynamic indentation,
 a indentation style where each nested if-statement is indented just far enough that it begins directly one empty space after the opening brace of the preceding condition.
 Unlike fixed indentation lengths,
 dynamic indentation adapts to the length of each condition individually.
 This experiment investigates if reading time is affected by the length of the words used in the conditions and by the nesting level,
 when dynamic indentation is used instead of a fixed indentation length.
\end_layout

