noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

),
 and chained conditions experiment (see Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Discussion-1_Chained_Condtions_Preliminary"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

),
 where nesting level showed a U-shaped pattern.
 This suggests that dynamic indentation may eliminate,
 or at least substantially reduce,
 the influence that nesting level had under fixed indentation,
 where reaction times followed a pronounced inverted U-shaped pattern depending on the position of the highlighted condition.
 One possible explanation is that dynamic indentation provides a more direct visual path to each condition,
 regardless of its nesting level,
 whereas fixed indentation may have caused participants to expend additional effort locating conditions in the middle of the nested structure.
 Notably,
 the overall reaction times measured in this experiment (approximately 380–430 ms) were considerably lower than those observed in the previous experiment on fixed indentation (approximately 560–950 ms).
 While this experiment was not designed as a direct comparison between fixed and dynamic indentation,
 this difference may indicate that dynamic indentation allows for faster comprehension overall,
 independent of nesting level or word length.
 However,
 as no statistical test was conducted between the two experiments,
 this observation should be interpreted with caution and would need to be confirmed in a dedicated follow-up experiment directly comparing both indentation styles under identical conditions.
 The absence of a significant effect for condition word length is consistent with the expectation that dynamic indentation,
 by construction,
 compensates for varying condition lengths:
 since each nested if-statement is positioned directly after the preceding condition's opening brace,
 differences in word length are absorbed into the layout itself rather than affecting the reader's ability to locate the relevant condition.
 These findings support the idea that dynamic indentation may be a promising alternative to fixed indentation lengths,
 warranting further investigation in a experiment that directly compares both approaches under controlled conditions.
\end_layout

