\begin_layout Subsection
Discussion
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Discussion-1_Overlap-Preliminary-Experiment"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results show that both indentation length and the presence of overlap affect the reading time.
 Also these two factors interact strongly.
 Indentation lengths benefit from short non-overlapping conditions,
 but shorter indentation lengths means longer reaction time because conditions became long enough to visually overlap.
 Dynamic indentation,
 by contrast,
 showed almost no difference between the Overlap and No_Overlap treatments (a difference of only 0.5%).
 This is a central result of this experiment,
 but it requires some clarification regarding what "Overlap" means in the context of dynamic indentation.
 Since dynamic indentation always positions each nested if-statement directly after the preceding condition's opening brace,
 no visual overlap can occur under this indentation style,
 regardless of how long the condition words are.
 The Overlap treatment was nonetheless applied to dynamic indentation as well,
 using the same long word lengths as for the fixed indentation lengths,
 even though these words could not produce any actual visual overlap.
 In this sense,
 the "overlap" tested under dynamic indentation is not a true visual overlap,
 but rather a word-length manipulation applied without its usual visual consequence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This design choice was deliberate,
 and it is precisely what makes the comparison meaningful:
 it allows the word length used in the Overlap condition to be held constant across all indentation styles,
 isolating the effect of visual overlap itself from the mere effect of longer words.
 If the slowdown observed for fixed indentation lengths in the Overlap condition had been caused simply by the increased word length,
 one would expect a comparable slowdown for dynamic indentation as well,
 since the same long words were used there too.
 Instead,
 dynamic indentation showed virtually no difference between long and short condition words,
 while every fixed indentation length showed a substantial one.
 This indicates that the slowdown observed for fixed indentation lengths is not primarily driven by word length itself,
 but specifically by the visual overlap that long words produce once a fixed indentation step is used.
 Overlap,
 rather than word length in general,
 therefore appears to be the more direct cause of the increased reading time observed here.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This interpretation is further supported by a shift in the ranking of indentation styles depending on the presence of overlap.
 Without overlap,
 dynamic indentation was not the fastest option;
 both length 10 and length 14 outperformed it slightly,
 and only length 6 was clearly slower.
 Once overlap was introduced,
 however,
 this ranking reversed completely:
 dynamic indentation became the fastest style overall,
 ahead of every fixed indentation length,
 including 14.
 This shift illustrates the core trade-off between fixed and dynamic indentation:
 fixed indentation lengths can be marginally faster under favorable conditions,
 when condition words happen to be short,
 but they carry the risk of a substantial penalty whenever conditions turn out to be long.
 Dynamic indentation sacrifices little of this best-case performance while entirely avoiding the worst case,
 making it the more robust choice when the length of conditions in real code cannot be known or controlled in advance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Taken together,
 these findings support the central motivation of this thesis:
 that dynamic indentation is not simply a stylistic alternative to fixed indentation,
 but a structural solution to a specific readability problem,
 namely the visual overlap that can arise when condition length and indentation step size are mismatched.
 As this experiment was conducted with a single participant,
 these results should be interpreted as preliminary.
 They motivate a main experiment involving multiple participants,
 in which the robustness of dynamic indentation against varying condition lengths can be evaluated more.
\end_layout

