\begin_layout Standard
The time required to identify the correct answer can be quite short so the method used to measure reaction time must be correspondingly precise and fast.
 For this reason,
 measurement is done using a single key press on the keyboard.
 An interactive interface was used to generate and present the code snippets as plain text,
 with some syntax highlighting.
 The participant completes each task by pressing the number key corresponding to the value displayed next to the closing brace of the highlighted if-statement,
 representing the participant's answer to the task.
 With this method,
 only little time is spent between the participant identifying the answer and inputting the result.
 After a key is pressed,
 the given answer,
 the expected answer,
 and the reaction time are recorded and saved.
 The participant can then move on to the next task by pressing 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
texttt{[Enter]}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The reaction time in milliseconds is measured from the moment the participant switches to the task,
 by pressing 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
texttt{[Enter]}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 to the moment a number key is pressed.
 The time is measured individually for each task,
 with the timer restarting at the beginning of every task.
 Reaction times and answers are only recorded during the main experiment and not during the training phase.
 Participants were allowed to take breaks between tasks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike the preliminary experiments,
 in which the author was the only participant and therefore no verification of the given answer was necessary,
 this experiment additionally records whether the given answer matches the expected answer for each task.
 This allows correctness to be analyzed as an additional measure of task difficulty across indentation styles and nesting levels,
 and serves as a filter to identify participants who may not have completed the experiment as intended.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using the interface,
 participants completed the experiment without supervision.
 After the experiment,
 participants were asked to send the generated csv-file to the researcher.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The experiment was executed on 19 volunteers:
 nine undergraduate students,
 five professional software developers,
 three graduate students and two participants in other occupations.
 The ages ranged from 18 to over 40:
 five participants were between 18 and 24 years old,
 seven between 25 and 29,
 one between 30 and 34,
 five between 35 and 39,
 and one participant was 40 or older.
 Programming experience varied considerably:
 while some participants reported no formal programming background,
 others reported more than 5 years of experience.
 All participants possessed at least general programming knowledge,
 though the level of expertise varied across the sample.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Reaction times exceeding 15000 milliseconds were excluded from the analysis,
 as such durations were considered indicative of a lapse in attention or an interruption external to the task,
 rather than genuine reading or reasoning time.
 This threshold removed 11 of 3990 recorded tasks (0.28%).
 A statistical outlier criterion based on the interquartile range (values above Q3 + 1.5×IQR,
 corresponding to approximately 3570 ms) was also considered,
 but was not applied,
 as it would have removed 300 tasks (7.52%),
 a substantial share of which likely reflect genuinely slower but valid responses under more difficult treatment combinations (e.g.,
 short indentation lengths combined with central nesting levels) rather than attentional lapses.
