\series default
Although this experiment included considerably more participants (19) and trials (3979 valid tasks) than the preliminary N-of-1 experiments,
 the sample is still small for a breakdown into background groups (nine undergraduate students,
 five professionals,
 three graduate students,
 two others),
 and conclusions about differences between these subgroups should be treated with caution.
 Reaction times above 15000 ms were excluded (11 of 3990 tasks,
 0.28%),
 as such durations most likely reflect a lapse in attention rather than genuine reading time.
 A stricter,
 IQR-based criterion (values above Q3 + 1.5 × IQR,
 approximately 3570 ms) was considered but not applied,
 since it would have removed a substantial share of the data (300 tasks,
 7.52%),
 plausibly including many genuinely slow but valid responses under harder treatment combinations (e.g.
 short indentation lengths combined with central nesting levels).
 This decision means that some remaining variance may still be driven by occasional attentional lapses rather than by the manipulated variables.
 Given that the Subject effect was itself significant and interacted with both Indentation_Length and Nesting_Level,
 a per-subject outlier criterion (WIE ICH OBEN BEREITS EVENTUELL GESAGT HABE),
 as opposed to one global threshold applied across the whole sample,
 could yield a different picture and should be considered as a sensitivity check in future analyses.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Labeling
\labelwidthstring 00.00.0000

\series bold
Participants 
\series default
Participants differed considerably in occupation,
 age and programming background:
 ages ranged from 18 to over 40,
 and programming experience ranged from none to more than five years.
 Status (undergraduate,
 graduate,
 professional,
 other) was recorded but treated as a non-controlled variable rather than a factor in the main analysis,
 and participants were recruited as volunteers rather than sampled randomly from a defined population.
 Because both the main effect of Subject and its interactions with Indentation_Length and Nesting_Level were significant,
 it is likely that some of this variability,
 and possibly the magnitude of the treatment effects,
 differs systematically between these groups;
 the reported average effects may therefore not generalize equally to all subgroups,
 e.g.
 inexperienced versus highly experienced programmers.
 It should also be noted that Age and Status are not independent in this sample:
 undergraduate students were predominantly in the younger age brackets (18–24,
 25–29),
 while professional developers and part of the graduate students skewed toward the older brackets (35 and above).
 Two of the five Age brackets (30–34 and 40 or older) contain only a single participant each,
 meaning any apparent difference for these brackets reflects that one individual rather than a group tendency,
 and the resulting confidence intervals for these two brackets are visibly wider and less stable than for the other three.
\end_layout

