\begin_layout Subsection
Discussion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results of the main experiment confirm and extend the findings from the preceding preliminary experiments in a considerably larger and more heterogeneous sample.
 Both Indentation_Length (p < .001,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{2}_{p}$
\end_inset

 = .167) and Nesting_Level (p < .001,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{2}_{p}$
\end_inset

 = .237) had a significant,
 medium-to-large effect on reaction time,
 and their interaction was significant again (p < .001,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{2}_{p}$
\end_inset

 = .122),
 replicating the pattern already seen in the Baseline Indentation Experiment (Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Analysis"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

):
 short indentation lengths amplify the cost of nesting level,
 and this cost flattens out as the indentation step increases.
 At an indentation length of 2,
 the difference between the slowest nesting level (4) and the fastest (7) was 173.8%,
 compared to 61.5% at a length of 8 and only 54.9% for dynamic indentation,
 the smallest gap of any treatment.
 This supports the interpretation already put forward for the preliminary experiments,
 that a longer indentation step specifically reduces the disproportionate cost of reading conditions near the center of the nested structure,
 rather than shifting reaction times uniformly across all levels.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As in the Baseline Experiment,
 Nesting_Level followed an inverted U-shape,
 but with the peak now at level 4 instead of the center of a nine-level structure,
 a consequence of the reduced maximum depth of seven levels used here.
 Level 4 was 70.6% slower than level 1 and 91.4% slower than level 7,
 while levels 1 and 2 did not differ significantly (p = .505),
 nor did levels 3 and 6 (p = .449) a near-symmetric pattern around the center that again suggests reaction time is driven by a condition's distance from the vertical center of the snippet rather than by its absolute nesting depth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The effect of Indentation_Length itself was substantial but non-monotonic.
 Most of the improvement occurred between the shorter lengths (2→4:
 16.7%;
 4→6:
 17.2%),
 while the gain from 6 to 8 was much smaller (7.4%),
 and length 10 was even 6.6% slower than length 8,
 showing again the same reversal already observed between lengths 4 and 6 in the Chained Conditions Experiment (Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Analysis-1_Chained_Condtions_Preliminary"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

).
 Post-hoc comparisons (Table 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
ref{tab:PostHoc_IndentationLength}
\end_layout

\end_inset

) show this reversal is not statistically robust:
 none of the pairwise comparisons among 6,
 8 and 10 reached clear significance (6 vs.
 8:
 p = .050;
 6 vs.
 10:
 p = 1.000;
 8 vs.
 10:
 p = .112),
 suggesting that once an indentation length of roughly 6 is reached,
 further increases neither reliably help nor hurt reading time in this design,
 except for dynamic indentation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Dynamic indentation was the fastest treatment overall and differed significantly from every fixed length (all p < .001).
 This result,
 however,
 needs a more cautious interpretation than in the preliminary experiments.
 Unlike the Dynamic Indentation Preliminary Experiment (Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Dynamic-Indentation-Preliminary"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) or the Overlap Preliminary Experiment (Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Overlap-Preliminary-Experiment"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

),
 where condition word length varied in a way dynamic indentation adapted to that variation conditions in this experiment always consist of exactly two four-character words joined by the same characters which means that every nesting level is shifted by exactly 19 characters ( 3 for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

if(
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

 + 12 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

condition
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 + 3 from 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

) {
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

 + 1 blank space) regardless of which words are drawn.
 Because word length was deliberately held constant to keep conditions comparable across nesting levels and treatments,
 "Dynamic" indentation in this experiment is mathematically indistinguishable from an additional fixed indentation length of 19.
 Its advantage over the tested fixed lengths (2–10) is therefore consistent with the general pattern that longer indentation continues to help reading time,
 rather than demonstrating any content-adaptive property but still overlap avoiding property.
 This experiment therefore cannot distinguish whether dynamic indentation performed best because it avoids overlap to content,
 or simply because its resulting offset happens to exceed the largest fixed length tested.
 Holding word length constant was nonetheless a deliberate and arguably necessary trade-off,
 since only conditions of comparable length allow indentation length and nesting level to be compared fairly across treatments.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The accuracy analysis (Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Accuracy-Analysis"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) supports the reaction time results rather than suggesting a speed–accuracy trade-off.
 The error rate was also highest at an indentation length of 2 (7.6%,
 more than five times higher than at length 4 or 8) and lowest for dynamic indentation (0.5%),
 and it also peaked at the central nesting levels (3,
 4 and 5,
 each above 3%) while remaining below 1% at the outer levels.
 Since both accuracy and reaction time become worse under the same conditions,
 it is unlikely that participants simply traded speed for accuracy under short indentation lengths;
 rather,
 both measures point to a genuine increase in task difficulty when indentation is short and the target condition lies near the center of the structure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 the significant effect of Subject (p < .001,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{2}_{p}$
\end_inset

 = .220) and its significant interactions with Indentation_Length (
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{2}_{p}$
\end_inset

 = .089),
 Nesting_Level (
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{2}_{p}$
\end_inset

 = .101) and their combination (
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{2}_{p}$
\end_inset

 = .180) indicate that participants differed considerably in how strongly indentation length and nesting level affected their reading speed.
 A closer look at the per-participant results (Tables 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
ref{tab:per_subject_length_1_10}
\end_layout

\end_inset

–
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
ref{tab:per_subject_level_11_20}
\end_layout

\end_inset

),
 however suggests that background variables alone cannot account for most of this variability.
 The participant with the strongest Indentation_Length effect (Participant 14,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{2}_{p}$
\end_inset

 = .620) belonged to the same Status group as several participants showing no effect at all,
 while participants within the same group varied almost as widely among themselves:
 among the nine undergraduate students alone,
 three (Participants 1,
 3 and 20) showed no significant Indentation_Length effect,
 while others reached large effect sizes (e.g.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{2}_{p}$
\end_inset

 = .620 for Participant 14).
 This indicates that the significant Subject effect is driven at least as much by variability within each background group as by systematic differences between groups.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One pattern nonetheless recurs consistently across Figures 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Interaction-diagram-Indentation_Length_Status"
