reference "fig:Interaction-diagram-Nesting_Level_Age"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

:
 participants in the "Other" Status category were on average the slowest across both Indentation_Length and Nesting_Level,
 and included one of the two participants for whom Indentation_Length was not significant.
 In the Age-based diagrams,
 a comparable standout appears for the 30–34 bracket,
 which shows the slowest reaction times of any Age group at the longer indentation lengths and at the peak of the Nesting_Level curve (level 4),
 while the oldest bracket (40 or older) does not stand out in the same way and is in fact the fastest group under dynamic indentation.
 Because the "Other" group comprises only two participants and the 30–34 and 40-or-older Age brackets only one participant each,
 these patterns are better treated as individual outliers than as evidence of a genuine Status or Age effect.
 Taken together,
 these results suggest that the apparent slowdowns visible for specific Status or Age subgroups are more consistent with individual variation among the one or two participants who happen to populate those subgroups than with a systematic effect of occupational background or age,
 reinforcing the conclusion that most of the observed inter-individual variability likely stems from other,
 unmeasured sources of individual difference such as prior exposure to particular code styles or general reading speed that this experiment was not designed to isolate.
\end_layout

